WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.020 --> 00:00:08.690
So I'm here to talk about
enterprise level data

00:00:08.690 --> 00:00:11.540
science success.

00:00:11.540 --> 00:00:14.550
It's making data scientist
success in a large company.

00:00:14.550 --> 00:00:17.180
That's what I want
to talk to you about.

00:00:17.180 --> 00:00:18.030
Who am I?

00:00:18.030 --> 00:00:21.740
I've used R for about six years.

00:00:21.740 --> 00:00:24.710
I come from an
engineering background.

00:00:24.710 --> 00:00:28.190
In my professional role, I
lead a data science, analytics

00:00:28.190 --> 00:00:32.360
and software team in the
supply chain organization.

00:00:32.360 --> 00:00:34.117
The silver lining of
the last two years

00:00:34.117 --> 00:00:36.200
is I don't have to explain
what supply chain means

00:00:36.200 --> 00:00:39.900
to anybody, from toilet
paper to semiconductor chips.

00:00:39.900 --> 00:00:43.340
Everybody understand
what supply chain means.

00:00:43.340 --> 00:00:47.180
Flex is a large electronics
manufacturing company.

00:00:47.180 --> 00:00:48.800
It's a Fortune 500 company.

00:00:48.800 --> 00:00:50.850
It's located in 30
different countries.

00:00:50.850 --> 00:00:52.280
We manufacture
electronics things

00:00:52.280 --> 00:00:55.970
in 30 different countries.

00:00:55.970 --> 00:00:57.450
Outside of work,

00:00:57.450 --> 00:01:00.950
my primary role is a
dad to two young kids,

00:01:00.950 --> 00:01:05.300
and mom currently thinks I'm on
vacation in a fancy conference

00:01:05.300 --> 00:01:08.030
resort.

00:01:08.030 --> 00:01:09.240
The session was themed,

00:01:09.240 --> 00:01:10.690
working with people is hard.

00:01:10.690 --> 00:01:13.210
It is hard.

00:01:13.210 --> 00:01:16.630
I agree that it is
not easy, but I'm here

00:01:16.630 --> 00:01:20.380
to try and convince you a
little bit that it need not

00:01:20.380 --> 00:01:21.060
be this hard.

00:01:24.840 --> 00:01:29.150
So what does data science
success in an enterprise mean?

00:01:29.150 --> 00:01:31.770
I'm going to give
you one definition.

00:01:31.770 --> 00:01:35.010
That's my definition.

00:01:35.010 --> 00:01:38.110
When there is effort that
leads to some output.

00:01:38.110 --> 00:01:39.720
And then there
are decisions made

00:01:39.720 --> 00:01:43.650
with that output and actions
and outcome for an organization

00:01:43.650 --> 00:01:46.620
or an enterprise.

00:01:46.620 --> 00:01:49.860
So we know what the effort, the
tools that we use in the effort

00:01:49.860 --> 00:01:52.820
and the outputs are
we use R and Python.

00:01:52.820 --> 00:01:56.450
We publish our outputs.

00:01:56.450 --> 00:01:58.020
Now for the second part.

00:01:58.020 --> 00:02:01.910
Decisions are generally made
with other people in meetings,

00:02:01.910 --> 00:02:05.240
whether it be with
higher ups or your peers

00:02:05.240 --> 00:02:08.900
or with people who will, who
are the practitioners and users

00:02:08.900 --> 00:02:11.940
of the output that we provide.

00:02:11.940 --> 00:02:13.890
And actions and
outcomes typically

00:02:13.890 --> 00:02:17.410
mean we make more sales,
we make more revenue.

00:02:17.410 --> 00:02:20.460
The key performance
indicators show improvement

00:02:20.460 --> 00:02:23.670
after implementing
the analytics.

00:02:23.670 --> 00:02:25.380
My talk is going to
be centered around

00:02:25.380 --> 00:02:32.910
the third and fourth phases of
making data science success.

00:02:32.910 --> 00:02:36.720
And in most organizations,
data science and analytics

00:02:36.720 --> 00:02:38.720
is a means to an end.

00:02:38.720 --> 00:02:39.800
It is.

00:02:39.800 --> 00:02:43.820
Success is measured by
dollars and metrics of what

00:02:43.820 --> 00:02:45.680
is important to the business.

00:02:45.680 --> 00:02:50.360
So it's a means to
an end in most cases.

00:02:50.360 --> 00:02:56.450
I see four sets as very
fundamental to data

00:02:56.450 --> 00:02:58.550
science success in a corporation.

00:02:58.550 --> 00:03:02.480
It's the data set, the
toolset, and the skill set

00:03:02.480 --> 00:03:04.950
is talked about a lot
in this conference.

00:03:04.950 --> 00:03:06.630
We have different
tools that we have.

00:03:06.630 --> 00:03:09.390
We improve our
skills. In this talk,

00:03:09.390 --> 00:03:11.960
I'm going to talk
more on the mindset

00:03:11.960 --> 00:03:16.850
within an organization of how
to take it from development

00:03:16.850 --> 00:03:20.420
deployment, where the first
two boxes that you saw

00:03:20.420 --> 00:03:23.180
implementation
actioning and outcome

00:03:23.180 --> 00:03:28.220
is what the mindset change that
is required for the last two

00:03:28.220 --> 00:03:28.986
processes.

00:03:32.060 --> 00:03:36.510
Partnership. This is a
word that means a lot.

00:03:36.510 --> 00:03:39.390
And I emphasize this
within the organization.

00:03:39.390 --> 00:03:42.200
There is a tendency to
treat data science and IT

00:03:42.200 --> 00:03:44.180
organizations as
a service provider

00:03:44.180 --> 00:03:47.260
within an organization.

00:03:47.260 --> 00:03:52.240
My passionate recommendation
is to not settle for this.

00:03:52.240 --> 00:03:53.890
Ask for equal.

00:03:53.890 --> 00:03:56.050
Ask to be equal partners.

00:03:56.050 --> 00:04:00.820
And what that means
is the data science

00:04:00.820 --> 00:04:03.340
team gets equal input
to key decisions that

00:04:03.340 --> 00:04:07.240
are made with the analysis
that they get to offer opinion,

00:04:07.240 --> 00:04:10.990
they get to guide the non
datas and the business people

00:04:10.990 --> 00:04:13.910
to execute on the output.

00:04:13.910 --> 00:04:17.690
And in return, the
data science team

00:04:17.690 --> 00:04:19.890
also owns the business outcome.

00:04:19.890 --> 00:04:21.829
So we take on an
additional challenge

00:04:21.829 --> 00:04:26.070
of owning business outcomes
and not just model deployment.

00:04:29.510 --> 00:04:33.590
So in terms of maturity
of an organization

00:04:33.590 --> 00:04:37.040
to utilize and
implement data science,

00:04:37.040 --> 00:04:39.440
I think of these
as two dimensional.

00:04:39.440 --> 00:04:42.710
One is the analytics maturity
or data science maturity.

00:04:42.710 --> 00:04:44.690
This could be
ingesting large amounts

00:04:44.690 --> 00:04:46.940
of data running complex models.

00:04:46.940 --> 00:04:50.660
The other important vector
is decision maturity.

00:04:50.660 --> 00:04:52.850
Is the organization.

00:04:52.850 --> 00:04:56.280
How good is the data
literacy of the organization?

00:04:56.280 --> 00:04:58.280
How good are they
in decision making

00:04:58.280 --> 00:05:00.560
with the uncertain
outputs that we have?

00:05:00.560 --> 00:05:04.820
Both of these vectors will
have to grow for data science

00:05:04.820 --> 00:05:06.980
to be successful
in an organization,

00:05:06.980 --> 00:05:10.760
and they have to go
grow simultaneously

00:05:10.760 --> 00:05:17.160
and both have to mature
for it to be a success.

00:05:17.160 --> 00:05:21.270
Decision maturity
is a fancy term

00:05:21.270 --> 00:05:25.110
for saying have a decision
framework to utilize the output

00:05:25.110 --> 00:05:28.900
that is given from analytics.

00:05:28.900 --> 00:05:31.930
And for the data
science team, it

00:05:31.930 --> 00:05:36.730
is important to understand
what are the key metrics that

00:05:36.730 --> 00:05:39.760
are important for the
practitioner of the analytics

00:05:39.760 --> 00:05:43.060
or what's important
for the business?

00:05:43.060 --> 00:05:46.480
And we talk a lot about model
metrics, model accuracy,

00:05:46.480 --> 00:05:50.500
classification error,
whatever the case might be.

00:05:50.500 --> 00:05:54.880
The model metrics only need
to beat the current business

00:05:54.880 --> 00:05:58.480
process metric to
be implementable.

00:05:58.480 --> 00:06:00.100
It's a two horse race.

00:06:00.100 --> 00:06:03.190
Model accuracy does
not need to be at 99%

00:06:03.190 --> 00:06:06.220
to be implemented if the
business process metric is

00:06:06.220 --> 00:06:08.800
today at 70%. You don't
have to beat the world

00:06:08.800 --> 00:06:10.280
record to beat the other horse.

00:06:10.280 --> 00:06:11.920
You just have to
beat the other horse

00:06:11.920 --> 00:06:17.643
and then make decisions to
implement the data science

00:06:17.643 --> 00:06:19.560
side of things on the
business side of things.

00:06:23.140 --> 00:06:27.400
This is contradictory
to, you know,

00:06:27.400 --> 00:06:28.970
working nicely with people.

00:06:28.970 --> 00:06:30.910
It's important to
pick some fights

00:06:30.910 --> 00:06:34.540
and you have to fight
for the right reasons.

00:06:34.540 --> 00:06:38.290
Friction is a very necessary
component to moving forward.

00:06:38.290 --> 00:06:40.210
Good friction is good.

00:06:40.210 --> 00:06:43.150
That removes hurdles
and blockades

00:06:43.150 --> 00:06:45.613
that sometimes come when
different teams have

00:06:45.613 --> 00:06:47.280
to work together to
move things forward.

00:06:51.800 --> 00:06:54.350
I want to make an
illustration or an example

00:06:54.350 --> 00:06:57.280
of how do we go from model
output to decision making?

00:06:57.280 --> 00:07:01.130
You see a picture of Google
Maps that takes us from where

00:07:01.130 --> 00:07:04.310
we are today to Dulles Airport.

00:07:04.310 --> 00:07:07.880
Many of us will be making this
trip in the next 24 hours.

00:07:07.880 --> 00:07:12.690
Google Maps tells me it's
going to take 45 minutes.

00:07:12.690 --> 00:07:14.640
I ask the question
to Google Maps,

00:07:14.640 --> 00:07:15.970
how long is it going to take?

00:07:15.970 --> 00:07:19.110
But that's not
the real question.

00:07:19.110 --> 00:07:21.990
When I have to make a decision
with the answer it's given me,

00:07:21.990 --> 00:07:26.670
the decision making breaks
down into multiple tree type

00:07:26.670 --> 00:07:28.690
decision making.

00:07:28.690 --> 00:07:31.210
Do I want to take Uber
or can I afford to

00:07:31.210 --> 00:07:32.830
take public transportation?

00:07:32.830 --> 00:07:34.720
Do I have time for coffee?

00:07:34.720 --> 00:07:38.170
When you put this
decision making framework

00:07:38.170 --> 00:07:40.450
on top of the very
complex analytics that's

00:07:40.450 --> 00:07:42.670
running behind Google
Maps, that gives you

00:07:42.670 --> 00:07:45.520
a framework to connect

00:07:45.520 --> 00:07:48.500
data science to business
type decision making.

00:07:48.500 --> 00:07:51.190
Talk to your stakeholders
to understand,

00:07:51.190 --> 00:07:52.630
what decisions do
they really want

00:07:52.630 --> 00:07:55.960
to make with the data output?

00:07:55.960 --> 00:08:00.360
Going from a generic
example, I'll go to a

00:08:00.360 --> 00:08:02.610
supply chain and
a specific example.

00:08:02.610 --> 00:08:04.530
Within the supply
chain organization,

00:08:04.530 --> 00:08:07.230
we have analytics
that support, that

00:08:07.230 --> 00:08:10.030
provide output for what the
price of a part should be.

00:08:10.030 --> 00:08:14.680
My organization and company buy
millions of parts every year.

00:08:14.680 --> 00:08:19.200
So the current, you can see
the scale of the pricing.

00:08:19.200 --> 00:08:22.710
When we provide most
of model outputs

00:08:22.710 --> 00:08:24.960
that show lower
than current price,

00:08:24.960 --> 00:08:29.430
the person who has to execute
on it when they first see it,

00:08:29.430 --> 00:08:31.770
they get some pressure.
They get some pressure

00:08:31.770 --> 00:08:34.419
or fear that they have
to go meet that target.

00:08:34.419 --> 00:08:40.740
And that pressure or fear is not
within that one person's scope,

00:08:40.740 --> 00:08:43.590
but their manager
also sees this data.

00:08:43.590 --> 00:08:45.910
So that's where the
pressure comes from.

00:08:45.910 --> 00:08:50.040
So there is a very high
chance that giving analytics

00:08:50.040 --> 00:08:53.430
in an organization can
create some pressure or fear.

00:08:53.430 --> 00:08:58.080
However, if the person
were to achieve any lower

00:08:58.080 --> 00:09:00.240
price for that part,
whether it meets that,

00:09:00.240 --> 00:09:02.940
regardless of whether it
meets the target or not,

00:09:02.940 --> 00:09:04.210
that's an opportunity.

00:09:04.210 --> 00:09:07.950
So you it is very important
to work within an organization

00:09:07.950 --> 00:09:11.280
to change people's
perception of analytics

00:09:11.280 --> 00:09:15.540
from feeling pressure or fear
to creating that opportunity

00:09:15.540 --> 00:09:18.270
and growth mindset.

00:09:18.270 --> 00:09:21.030
So we call it the pressure
to opportunity mindset change

00:09:21.030 --> 00:09:23.300
and it takes time when you
work with different teams.

00:09:26.900 --> 00:09:31.040
And humans tend to judge
machines and models very

00:09:31.040 --> 00:09:31.740
harshly.

00:09:31.740 --> 00:09:35.120
I'll give two scenarios with
this exact case of pricing

00:09:35.120 --> 00:09:36.710
a part.

00:09:36.710 --> 00:09:39.560
Now let's take the case
of the part was the dollar

00:09:39.560 --> 00:09:44.660
and the model output was $0.90
and the actual achievement

00:09:44.660 --> 00:09:47.690
after the negotiation was $0.95.

00:09:47.690 --> 00:09:52.640
In in case one or scenario
one with the model,

00:09:52.640 --> 00:09:54.230
people tend to judge
the model and say

00:09:54.230 --> 00:09:57.790
the model has 50% error.

00:09:57.790 --> 00:09:59.530
In case number two
or scenario two

00:09:59.530 --> 00:10:02.270
without a machine or a model.

00:10:02.270 --> 00:10:06.120
And when a human went in with
a low bid as a starting point.

00:10:06.120 --> 00:10:10.640
And they started with $0.90 and
got up to $0.95 as the result,

00:10:10.640 --> 00:10:12.360
it was a good
negotiating strategy.

00:10:12.360 --> 00:10:16.980
So humans tend to judge machines
and models in a very harsh way.

00:10:16.980 --> 00:10:19.760
We have a lot of empathy
towards other humans.

00:10:19.760 --> 00:10:24.650
So what we have learned
in doing analytics

00:10:24.650 --> 00:10:28.100
at scale within a
large company is

00:10:28.100 --> 00:10:30.830
be aware of being judged
in a very harsh way

00:10:30.830 --> 00:10:32.100
and prepare for that.

00:10:35.210 --> 00:10:40.170
So with people, there's
building relationships

00:10:40.170 --> 00:10:43.130
with relationships with
people up, down and sideways.

00:10:43.130 --> 00:10:44.010
Up the management

00:10:44.010 --> 00:10:50.010
chain, with chain with
your teams and your peers.

00:10:50.010 --> 00:10:53.230
The data science and analytics
team to be successful,

00:10:53.230 --> 00:10:57.000
have to learn to understand
and talk the language

00:10:57.000 --> 00:10:59.520
of the business teams.

00:10:59.520 --> 00:11:05.370
When we say models and
random forest and regression.

00:11:05.370 --> 00:11:07.900
Everybody does not
understand those terms.

00:11:07.900 --> 00:11:11.490
It makes them a little
closed and feared.

00:11:11.490 --> 00:11:14.430
So we have to open up and
learn to talk their language

00:11:14.430 --> 00:11:17.340
to get how they can
execute and do their jobs

00:11:17.340 --> 00:11:20.710
better with what
we are giving them.

00:11:20.710 --> 00:11:22.690
And building a
successful team takes

00:11:22.690 --> 00:11:27.030
time. Manage, lead, and coach are

00:11:27.030 --> 00:11:29.400
three different
things and all three

00:11:29.400 --> 00:11:32.240
have to be done in
a different way.

00:11:32.240 --> 00:11:36.950
And similarly, as we
challenge the non datas

00:11:36.950 --> 00:11:41.120
to speak our language, we have
to challenge the data science

00:11:41.120 --> 00:11:45.530
team in an organization to
speak the business team's

00:11:45.530 --> 00:11:46.030
language.

00:11:49.010 --> 00:11:53.180
Some pitfalls that
I've learned over time.

00:11:53.180 --> 00:11:56.340
When management asks for

00:11:56.340 --> 00:11:59.040
machine learning, whenever
they start a question

00:11:59.040 --> 00:12:01.650
is, let's see if
AI/ML can help here.

00:12:01.650 --> 00:12:05.010
That's usually a rabbit hole.

00:12:05.010 --> 00:12:07.630
Can we find something
interesting from the data?

00:12:07.630 --> 00:12:10.620
That's another big rabbit hole.

00:12:10.620 --> 00:12:14.170
Be aware of scope creep
in an organization.

00:12:14.170 --> 00:12:17.160
Have a clear plan
and a threshold

00:12:17.160 --> 00:12:20.530
for aborting a project with
your project stakeholders.

00:12:25.610 --> 00:12:28.790
So working with people
need not be this hard.

00:12:28.790 --> 00:12:31.820
Working with people is a
very necessary component

00:12:31.820 --> 00:12:33.565
of enterprise
level data success.

00:12:36.940 --> 00:12:39.360
So thank you.

